From 080b81bbb2b3068a8715e820807c4fbfdfa23819 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Sergey Dushenkov <ss.dushenkov@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 4 Nov 2026 23:08:26 +0300
Subject: [PATCH 2/5] sim: opt-in store buffering (x86-TSO) memory model

Atomic loads in the simulator always read the latest value, so
store -> load reordering is never observed: a missing seq_cst fence in
a Dekker-style protocol ("announce sleeping; fence; re-check" vs
"publish; fence; check sleeping") is a lost wake-up on real hardware,
but passes in simulation.

With SimulatorParams::store_buffering (default: CMake option
TWIST_SIM_STORE_BUFFERING, OFF) every thread gets a FIFO store buffer:

- relaxed / release stores are buffered, seq_cst stores drain it,
- loads forward from the own buffer, otherwise the scheduler decides
  (RandomChoice) whether pending stores of other threads to the
  location are flushed (with the older stores of the same buffer)
  before the load; a store missed by store_buffer_max_skips loads is
  flushed (eventual visibility, terminates spin loops under DFS),
- RMW operations (including failed CAS), seq_cst fences and system
  calls (futex, yield, sleep, spawn, exit) drain the buffer; before a
  system call the drain is a separate preemptible step,
- mutex::unlock is a seq_cst store (it reads contention_ afterwards),
- a pending store is flushed when its atomic is destroyed.

Flush decisions are ordinary scheduler decisions: explored by DFS,
recorded by Recorder and reproduced by Replay.

With store buffering off the only cost is a predictable branch in
the store / load / RMW paths and in the draining system calls.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
